/**
 * Raised when a create fails before the chat exists and before its first
 * message is admitted.
 *
 * `createThread` resolves a workspace, builds a session, asks the host for
 * that session's resource loader and applies the first-message rule, all
 * before Pi is handed the message — and Pi writes no session file until a
 * session has an assistant message. So every failure in that stage proves the
 * same two things: no chat was made, and no message got through. The stage is
 * what the type names, which is why a host-supplied loader that throws answers
 * exactly like the rule this class was first written for; the refusal used to
 * be the only failure there that said so, and the rest were read as outcomes
 * nobody could name.
 *
 * A `PiRequestError` fixed at `delivered: false`, because that answer is the
 * same however a host reaches it: over HTTP a route turns it into a 4xx and
 * the browser client reads the refusal off the status, and in process
 * `createPiNodeClient` forwards this error untouched. The original failure
 * rides on `cause`, and the message stays a plain sentence rather than the
 * cause's own — a host that shows it to a user is showing it to a user.
 *
 * A failure of the ADMISSION itself is not one of these: Pi has the message by
 * then, and nobody can say what became of it.
 */
